  "worked_solution": "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$C$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because **multiplication of $f\\in C$ by a scalar is not closed.** *\n\nFor example, if $\\alpha =-1$ and $f(x)={x}^{2}+1,$ then $\\alpha f(x)=-f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of $f,g\\in C$ is associative. $\\,\u2713$\n\n- $C$ contains the zero element. Indeed, the function $e(x),$ such that $e(x)=0$ for all $x\\in R,$ belongs to $C.$ This function is the zero element of $C$ since $e(x)+f(x)=f(x)+e(x)=f(x)$ for any $f(x)\\in C.$ $\\,\u2713$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in C.$ $\\,\u2713$\n\n- Addition of $f,g\\in C$ is commutative. $\\,\u2713$",
